AB  - This study was designed to investigate the effect of intense training on muscle glycogen stores under conditions of controlled carbohydrate (CHO) intake. On two separate occasins, 10 highly trained distnce runners increased their training load for 5 days (20 km/day, .apprx. 80% maximal O2 consumption) while eating a diet whose carbohydrate composition either equaled (EQ-CHO) or contained .apprx. 50% of the runner''s estimated daily expenditure (LO-CHO). Total muscle glycogen levels were lower after the  LO-CHO regimen. Photometric analysis of the glycogen content in individual fibers revealed that 27% type I and 17% type II fibers had optical densities < 0.2 U after the LO-CHO regimen, whereas 7% type I and 0% type II were similarly depleted after the EQ-CHO diet. A linear relationship was observed between the histochemical and direct chemical analysis of muscle glycogen content. Treadmill O2 uptake measured at 185 and 238 m/min was higher during the LO-CHO than the EQ-CHO regimen. Ratings of percieved exertion were higher during the 238-m/min run for LD-CHO regimen. After 3 days of rest, running economy and perception of effort returned to pretraining levels and muscle glycogen stores were .apprx. 85% of the pretraining values. Thus when CHO intake was only .apprx. 50% of the energy requirements there was a marked depletion of muscle glycogen stores, particularly in type I fibers, and a concomitant decrease in running economy and increased perception of fatigue. However, a CHO intake of 8.0 g .cntdot. kg-1 .cntdot. day-1 did not completely prevent depletion of muscle glycogen stores, suggesting  either a need for a greater amount of CHO, or some limitation in the athlete''s ability to store the ingested CHO.
