TI  - CHANGES IN VO2 MAX AND RUNNING PERFORMANCE WITH TRAINING
T2  - EUROPEAN JOURNAL OF APPLIED PHYSIOLOGY
LA  - English
AB  - The response of .ovrhdot.VO2 max [maximal O2 consumption] and of running performance (805 and 3218 m) to the onset of training in untrained individuals and to an increase in the volume and intensity of training in well trained individuals was determined. In series A, .ovrhdot.VO2 max and performances of 12 previously untrained individuals were determined before and after 4 and 8 wk of training. In series B, performances, .ovrhdot.VO2 max and .ovrhdot.VO2 submax of 15 previously well trained runners were determined before and after 4 and 8 wk of controlled training. In series A, .ovrhdot.VO2 max increased during the first 4 wk of training but failed to increase further even in the presence of an increased training load (80 total km for the first 4 wk, 130 total km for the second 4 wk). Running performances improved throughout the training period. In series B, neither .ovrhdot.VO2 max nor .ovrhdot.VO2 submax changed but running performance improved throughout the experimental period. Not all of the improvement in running performance subsequent to training is apparently attributable to changes in .ovrhdot.VO2 max. Changes in running economy are probably not a likely explanation for performance improvement among previously well-trained runners. Physiological adaptations not integrated in the test of .ovrhdot.VO2 max, or improvement in pacing apparently contribute to training induced improvements in running performance.
