AB  - PurposeThe aim of this study was to compare the suitability of models for practical applications in training planning.MethodsWe tested six impulse-response models, including Banister's model (Model Ba), a variable dose-response model (Model Bu), and indirect-response models differing in the way they account or not for the effect of previous training on the ability to respond effectively to a given session. Data from 11 swimmers were collected during 61 wk across two competitive seasons. Daily training load was calculated from the number of pool-kilometers and dry land workout equivalents, weighted according to intensity. Performance was determined from 50-m trials done during training sessions twice a week. Models were ranked on the base of Aikaike's information criterion along with measures of goodness of fit.ResultsModels Ba and Bu gave the greatest Akaike weights, 0.339 & PLUSMN; 0.254 and 0.360 & PLUSMN; 0.296, respectively. Their estimates were used to determine the evolution of performance over time after a training session and the optimal characteristics of taper. The data of the first 20 wk were used to train these two models and predict performance for the after 8 wk (validation data set 1) and for the following season (validation data set 2). The mean absolute percentage error between real and predicted performance using Model Ba was 2.02% & PLUSMN; 0.65% and 2.69% & PLUSMN; 1.23% for validation data sets 1 and 2, respectively, and 2.17% & PLUSMN; 0.65% and 2.56% & PLUSMN; 0.79% with Model Bu.ConclusionsThe findings showed that although the two top-ranked models gave relevant approximations of the relationship between training and performance, their ability to predict future performance from past data was not satisfactory for individual training planning.
