KW  - EXERCISE
AB  - The resistive work of breathing against an external load during inspiration (WRI) was measured at the mouth, during sub-maximal exercise in healthy participants. This measure (which excludes the elastic work component) allows the relationship between resistive work and power, ventilation and exercise modality to be explored. A total of 45 adult participants with healthy lung function took part in a series of exercise protocols, in which the relationship between WRI, power of breathing, PRI and minute ventilation, (V) over dot(E) were assessed during rest, while treadmill walking or ergometer cycling, over a range of exercise intensities (up to 150 Watts) and ventilation rates (up to 48 L min(-1)) with applied constant resistive loads of 0.75 and 1.5 kPa.L.sec(-1). Resting WRI was 0.12 JL(-1) and PRI was 0.9 W. At each resistive load, independent of the breathing pattern or exercise mode, the WRI increased in a linear fashion at 20 mJ per litre of (V) over dot(E), while PRI increased exponentially. With increasing resistive load the work and power at any given (V) over dot(E) increased exponentially. Calculation of the power to work ratio during loaded breathing suggests that loads above 1.5 kPa. L. sec(-1) make the work of resistive breathing become inhibitive at even a moderate (V) over dot(E) (>30 L sec(-1)). The relationship between work done and power generated while breathing against resistive loads is independent of the exercise mode (cycling or walking) and that ventilation is limited by the work required to breathe, rather than an inability to maintain or generate power.
