AB  - Background Exercise training improves exercise tolerance (peak oxygen consumption, peakVO(2)) in patients with heart failure with preserved ejection fraction (HFpEF), but it remains unclear whether the effects are comparable between sexes. Objectives To evaluate sex-specific exercise training effects on peakVO(2) in HFpEF. Methods This pooled analysis integrates data from patients with chronic HFpEF randomized to different modes of exercise training or usual care in the OptimEx-Clin and Ex-DHF-trials. Sex differences in changes of peakVO(2) (absolute, relative, percent-predicted) from baseline to 3, 6, and 12 months of exercise training or usual care were compared using linear-mixed models. Results Among 498 patients (mean age 69.3 +/- 7.7 years, 62% women), 277 (57%) were randomized to exercise training. Exercise training significantly increased absolute, relative and percent-predicted peakVO(2) compared to usual care in women (all P < .001), but not in men (all P > .05). A significant sex difference in the training response was observed for percent-predicted peakVO2 (sex * group * time-interaction P = .037). Although mean within-group changes in percent-predicted peakVO(2) following exercise training were similar between women and men (4.7% [95% CI, 2.2%-7.2%] vs 3.0% [-.3% to 6.4%] at 12 months), usual care was associated with a decline in women (-3.7% [-6.5% to -.8%]) but not in men (1.4% [-2.0% to 4.7%]). Conclusion Exercise training improved percent-predicted peakVO(2) in women but not in men with HFpEF, a sex difference that was largely driven by differing responses following usual care. Since women are usually underrepresented in exercise programmes, more efforts should be made to increase their participation rates
