TI  - A COMPARISON OF THE TRAINING RESPONSES TO AEROBIC DANCE AND JOGGING IN COLLEGE FEMALES
T2  - MEDICINE & SCIENCE IN SPORTS & EXERCISE
LA  - English
AB  - To compare the physiological alterations that occur in college females as a result of a 7-wk jogging and aerobic dance-training program, 46 subjects (18-29 yr) volunteered to participate and included 15 dancers, 19 joggers and 12 controls. All subjects were given a pre- and post-.ovrhdot.VO2 max [maximum O2 uptake] treadmill test. The joggers and dancers trained 4 d[day]/wk, 30 min/d for 7 wk at an intensity that represented .apprx. 83 and 84% of their initial maximal heart rates, respectively. Both experimental groups significantly (P < 0.05) increased their .ovrhdot.VO2 max, .ovrhdot.VEmax [maximal expiratory ventilation] and maximal treadmill running times and significantly (P < 0.05) decreased their maximal heart rates as a result of the training. The control group showed no significant (P < 0.05) changes in any of the variables measured. Aerobic dance and jogging were equally effective (P < 0.05) exercise modalities for improving cardiorespiratory endurance when performed at similar intensities, frequencies, and durations.
