AB  - The relationship between half time of the O2 uptake on-response (t1/2 .ovrhdot.VO2on, s) and early blood lactate accumulation (.DELTA.Lab, mmol/l) at the onset of submaximal arm and/or leg exercise was the object of a cross-sectional study of sedentary subjects (S, n = 3), and kayakers (K, n = 8), and of a longitudinal study on 11 untrained subjects of specific arm vs. leg training. In supine arm cranking (.ovrhdot.W = 125 watts) S had an average t1/2 .ovrhdot.VO2on of 82 s and a .DELTA.Lab of 9.2 mmol .cntdot. l-1 compared to 47 .+-. 7 s and 4 .+-. 1.4 mmol .cntdot. l-1, respectively, for K. In longitudinal trainees shorter t1/2 .ovrhdot.VO2on was accompanied by lower Lab for the trained limbs. Specific limb conditioning in swimmers and runners resulted in shorter t1/2 .ovrhdot.VO2on. A linear relationship was observed between .DELTA.Lab and t1/2 .ovrhdot.VO2on having an intercept on the time axis at .simeq. 20 s and a slope proportional to muscle mass. Trained muscles were grouped closest to the intercept indicating local acceleration of the rate of O2 transfer approaching the t1/2 .ovrhdot.VO2on for isolated perfused muscle at the onset of work. Since t1/2 for local blood flow adjustment is shorter than t1/2 .ovrhdot.VO2on, factors distal to the capillary appear to be specifically involved in the local training response.
