T2  - JOURNAL OF APPLIED PHYSIOLOGY
LA  - English
AB  - Plasma glucagon and catecholamines increase during prolonged submaximal exercise, but the magnitude of the increase is less in endurance-trained individuals than in untrained subjects. The rapidity at which this adaptation occurs was studied. Six initially untrained healthy subjects exercised vigorously (on bicycle ergometers and by running) 30-50 min/day, 6 days/wk, for 9 wk. Prior to the beginning of training and at 3 wk intervals thereafter, participants were subjected to 90 min bycycle ergometer test work loads that elicited 58 .+-. 2% of the subjects'' initial maximal oxygen consumption. The major proportion of the training-induced decrement in plasma glucagon and catecholamine responses to exercise was seen after 3 wk of training. The hormonal component of the training adaptation occurs very early in the course of vigorous endurance training program.
